---
import Files from "@/components/builders/Files.astro";
import Flow from "@/components/builders/Flow.astro";
import Pager from "@/components/builders/Pager.astro";
import RecordedNote from "@/components/builders/RecordedNote.astro";
import Shots from "@/components/builders/Shots.astro";
import Transcript from "@/components/builders/Transcript.astro";
import CodeBlock from "@/components/CodeBlock.astro";
import Docs from "@/layouts/Docs.astro";
import { recordedOf } from "@/lib/builders";
import { docHref } from "@/lib/docs";
import { repoFile } from "@/lib/site";

const rec = recordedOf("user-code");
const f = rec.facts;
const raw = import.meta.glob<string>("../../builders/examples/user-code/**/*", {
  query: "?raw",
  import: "default",
  eager: true,
});
const files = Object.fromEntries(
  [
    "src/App.tsx",
    "src/main.tsx",
    "src/tasks.ts",
    "src/styles.css",
    "index.html",
    "package.json",
  ].map((p) => [p, raw[`../../builders/examples/user-code/${p}`]]),
);

const client = `// An editor, a playground or an agent: write files, snapshot, show the page.
const P = "http://localhost:8787/p/tasks";

await fetch(\`\${P}/import\`, {
  method: "POST",
  headers: { "content-type": "application/json" },
  body: JSON.stringify({ files }),          // { "src/App.tsx": "…", "package.json": "…", … }
});
const { snapshot, base } = await (await fetch(\`\${P}/snapshots\`, { method: "POST" })).json();
iframe.src = \`\${base}~/index.html\`;          // the app, compiled module by module

// After an edit: write the file, take a new snapshot, point the iframe at it.
await fetch(\`\${P}/files/src/App.tsx\`, { method: "PUT", body: newSource });
const next = await (await fetch(\`\${P}/snapshots\`, { method: "POST" })).json();
iframe.src = \`\${next.base}~/index.html\`;`;
---

<Docs
  title="User code as ESM"
  description="The platform's module server for a project's own files: TypeScript, TSX and CSS served as ES modules from immutable snapshots, npm imports bound to the ESM CDN. A working example with recorded results."
>
  <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-primary">Builders · 2 of 5</p>
  <h1>User code as ESM: a project, served as it is written</h1>
  <p>
    The ESM CDN serves packages others published. This builder serves the code you are writing:
    a project's TypeScript, TSX, JSX and CSS files, each compiled to an ES module when the browser
    asks for it. Write the files, take a snapshot, and open the snapshot's{" "}
    <code>index.html</code>. There is no bundling step and no dev server to keep running.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Editors and playgrounds in the browser.</strong> A change recompiles only the file
      that changed, when it is next requested.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>AI agents that write front-end code.</strong> The agent writes files over HTTP and
      gets a URL that runs them, with real npm packages.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Previews that do not move under you.</strong> Each snapshot has its own URLs. A page
      that is open keeps importing the version it started with, even after the next edit.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The input of the bundler.</strong> <code>/p/&lt;id&gt;/export/&lt;snapshot&gt;</code>{" "}
      is the snapshot as a ZIP, and <code>POST /builds?project=&lt;id&gt;&amp;snapshot=&lt;snapshot&gt;</code>{" "}
      builds it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <Flow
    steps={[
      { label: "PUT · POST /import", detail: "files go into the project's working tree", kind: "client" },
      { label: "POST /snapshots", detail: "the tree is frozen under the hash of its contents" },
      { label: "GET /s/<snap>/~/<path>", detail: "each file compiled to ESM on first request, then cached" },
      { label: "~deps/ → ESM CDN", detail: "npm imports re-export the CDN's pinned URLs", kind: "external" },
    ]}
    caption="One Project object per project. The project's own files are compiled here; npm packages are the CDN's, shared by every project."
  />
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>A working tree.</strong> <code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/import</code> replaces the tree
      with a ZIP or a JSON map of files; <code>PUT /p/&lt;id&gt;/files/&lt;path&gt;</code> writes
      one file. The tree is stored in the <code>Project</code> object's SQLite.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A snapshot.</strong> <code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/snapshots</code> copies the tree
      under the hash of its contents and returns its base URL. The same files give the same id:
      the recording's first snapshot is <code>{f.snapshot}</code>, and importing the same six
      files again anywhere gives it again.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A module per file.</strong> <code>GET &lt;base&gt;~/src/App.tsx</code> is compiled
      by webrun-modules: types stripped, JSX compiled with React's automatic runtime, CSS imports
      turned into <code>?module</code> imports (Lightning CSS lowers nesting), and every import
      made a URL (an import without an extension, <code>"./tasks"</code>, gets the file's,
      <code>"./tasks.ts"</code>). The answer is <code>immutable</code>, since a snapshot never
      changes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>npm imports bind to the CDN.</strong> <code>import "react"</code> becomes{" "}
      <code>../~deps/react/index.js</code>, a module in the snapshot that re-exports the CDN's
      pinned URL for the range in the project's <code>package.json</code>{" "}
      (<code>export * from "…/npm/browser/react@19.3.1/index.js"</code>). The project compiles
      only its own files; every project shares the CDN's packages.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Request</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/import</code></td><td>replaces the working tree: a ZIP, or <code>{`{ files: { path: text } }`}</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>PUT /p/&lt;id&gt;/files/&lt;path&gt;</code>, <code>DELETE …</code></td><td>writes or removes one file</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/snapshots</code></td><td><code>{`{ snapshot, base }`}</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GET /p/&lt;id&gt;/s/&lt;snap&gt;/~/&lt;path&gt;</code></td><td>a source as ESM; <code>index.html</code> as it is</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>POST /p/&lt;id&gt;/s/&lt;snap&gt;/resolve</code></td><td>entry URLs and the project modules to preload</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GET /p/&lt;id&gt;/live/…</code></td><td>302 to the latest snapshot, made on demand</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>GET /p/&lt;id&gt;/export/&lt;snap|live&gt;</code></td><td>the snapshot as a ZIP</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>Usage</h2>
  <p>
    The example is a small task list: React, a TSX component, a TypeScript helper that uses{" "}
    <code>nanoid</code> for ids, and nested CSS. Nothing in it is installed or built; the{" "}
    <code>package.json</code> only says which versions to use.
  </p>
  <Files
    files={files}
    source={{ label: "site/src/builders/examples/user-code/", href: repoFile("site/src/builders/examples/user-code/src/App.tsx") }}
  />
  <p>From a shell, in the example's folder:</p>
  <CodeBlock
    lang="sh"
    code={`node -e 'const fs=require("fs");const files={};for(const p of process.argv.slice(1))files[p]=fs.readFileSync(p,"utf8");console.log(JSON.stringify({files}))' index.html package.json src/* \\
  | curl -X POST -H 'content-type: application/json' --data-binary @- http://localhost:8787/p/tasks/import
curl -X POST http://localhost:8787/p/tasks/snapshots     # → { snapshot, base }: open <base>~/index.html`}
  />
  <p>From an app, the same three calls:</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="js" code={client} />

  <h2>Results</h2>
  <RecordedNote recorded={rec} />
  <h3>The page</h3>
  <Shots shots={rec.shots} />
  <h3>The requests</h3>
  <Transcript steps={rec.steps} />
  <p>
    After the edit the project has two snapshots, <code>{f.snapshot}</code> and{" "}
    <code>{f.snapshot2}</code>. The recorder checked that the first one still serves the
    original <code>src/main.tsx</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A project's <code>~deps</code> module for a CDN package emits <code>default</code> only
      when the importer asks for it, since the project does not analyse the CDN's exports.
    </li>
    <li>
      The transforms are per file (sucrase), with no type checking and no Vite plugins. For a
      framework build, use the <a href="/builders/astro">Astro builder</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    API reference: <a href={docHref("packages/esm-cdn/README.md")}>ESM CDN &amp; user code</a>.
  </p>
  <Pager slug="user-code" />
</Docs>
